(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2609102400-a-guard-aimed-at-a-shadowed-file")
  (:adr/title "A guard aimed at a shadowed file is no guard: six site sources have two copies and one renders")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609102200-https-required-and-generate-now"))
  (:adr/body
  "Follow-up to ADR-2609102200, which named one thing not done: a check that
fails when `llms.txt` and the docs page disagree. Writing that check found why
the repo's EXISTING guard had never fired.

## The shadowing

`src/cloud_murakumo/site/docs.cljc` was COPIED to `cloud-murakumo-app` on
2026-08-31, not moved. Both files still exist, and both directories are on the
`:site` classpath:

    1  src                                    <- this repo, wins
    2  resources
    ...
    7  .../cloud-murakumo-app/src             <- shadowed
    8  .../cloud-murakumo-app/resources

So this repo's copy renders and the other one is invisible. A previous fix
repointed `test/llms_public_api_test.mjs` at the -app copy, believing the file
had moved -- its comment says so. The result, measured 2026-09-10: `/docs` and
`/llms.txt` disagreed for an hour, the live page rendered from the copy the
test was NOT reading, and the assertions passed the whole time.

**A guard aimed at a shadowed file is not a weaker guard. It is no guard**, and
it is worse than none because it reports a pass.

## It is not one file

    file              changed lines between the two copies
    blog.cljc         632
    market.cljc       360
    go.cljc           296
    onprem_chat.cljc  275
    onprem.cljc       243
    chrome.cljc        52

Direction, measured from git rather than assumed: `cloud-murakumo` took **68
commits** touching `site/` in thirty days with every file current to 09-09;
`cloud-murakumo-app` took **16**, and those edits landed in files that never
render. Three files exist only in -app (`giemon.cljc`, `landing_locales.clj`,
`trust.clj`) and are therefore NOT shadowed -- those do render.

## What was changed, and what deliberately was not

Which repo owns the site source is an owner decision, and 1,858 lines of
divergence are not something a test should force. Nothing was moved or deleted.
What changed is that the problem can no longer be silent:

- `llms.txt` must name every model and every public route `/docs` names. That
  is the exact drift of 2026-09-10, and `llms.txt` is what an agent reads
  first, so a model documented only in HTML is undiscoverable to it.
- `llms.txt` must keep stating that HTTPS is required.
- A ratchet over the shadowed files (`test/shadowed-site-sources.json`): a NEW
  divergence fails; an entry that has become identical fails, so the list can
  only shrink; and an entry that exists on only one side fails.

That last rule came from testing the ratchet against itself. Adding an entry
for a file absent from the sibling passed **silently**, because the comparison
loop only walks files present in both -- so a stale entry would have sat there
forever. It is the same shape as the bug the ratchet exists to catch, one level
up, and it was caught only because the check was broken deliberately in both
directions before being trusted.

## Owner decision pending

Which repo owns `cloud_murakumo/site/`. Until then the seven listed files stay
listed, and every edit to the -app copy of one of them changes nothing.")))
